        "problem": "Example 1.22\n7 6 42 7\n10 9 90 15\nIf P(A)=0.60, P(B) = 0.40, P(A B) = 0.24. (i) What is the relation between A and B? Also find (ii) P (A) (iii) P(AUB) (iv) P(A/B).\nSolution:\n(1)\n(ii)\n(iii)\nHere P(A) P(B) = (0.60) (0.40) = 0.24 = P(AB). Multiplication law for independent events is satisfied. Thus, A and B are independent events.\nBy complementation law\nP(A)=1-P(A) = 1-0.60=0.40\nP(AUB) = P(A) + P(B)-P(AB)\n=0.60+0.40-0.24=0.76\n(iii) P(A/B)=\nP(ANB) 0.24\nP(B)\n0.60= P(A)\n0.40\n(This statement also shows that events A and B are independent).\n1.3.6 Odds for the occurrence of an event\nThe term \"odds\" refers to the ratio of two probabilities; the ratio of probability of success to the probability of failure. Any kind of probability can be\nexpressed as odds, defined as d = where d = odds and p = Probability of\nP 1-p\nsuccess. For example; in throwing a die the probability of 3 is one in six that is p\nThe odds of getting a 3 are d=\nP\n1/6 1/6 1 1-p 1-1/6 5/6 5\nThat is, odds for\n3 are 1 to 5. Odds are used by researchers in different fields.",
