          "rubric": "Use the textbook explanation, relevant notation and worked examples.",
          "sol": "Counting rules help us to know about the number of all possible results of an experiment without actually writing them. A few of the commonly used counting rules to solve the probability problems are:\nThe product of first n natural numbers is called factorial and is denoted by\nThe groups which can be made from a given number of objects by taking together some or all of the objects at a time with regard to the order of the objects are called permutations. For example, if there are three objects A, B and C, then permutations of size two will be AB, BA, AC, CA, BC, CB. Generally, if we have &quot;n&quot; objects, then total number of permutations of size &quot;&quot; can be obtained by the formula\nConsider the above example, n=3 (number of objects), r=2 (group size), therefore\nHow many different three digit numbers can be formed from the digits 3,\nPermutations when all objects are taken at a time (without repetition) When all of the given objects are considered at a time in the formation of groups i.e. rn then the total number of permutations = P=n!\nHow many different permutations can be made from the letters of the word &quot;BOXER&quot;?\nHere r = n = 5, therefore, total number of permutations= 51-5-4-3-2-1-120\nWhen all of the given objects are considered at a time in the formation of groups and out of them ni are of one kind, n2 are of second kind... n are of the kth kind, then the total number of permutations is given by\nHow many different permutations can be made from the letters of the word &quot;STATISTICS&quot;?"
