        "problem": "Example 8.4\nConsider the data given in the following contingency table.\nMathematical ability\nGood\nFair\nPoor\nGeneral ability\nB1\nB2\nB3\n44\n82\n44\nGood\n265\n257\n178\nFair\n41\n91\n98\nPoor\nDiscuss the association between the two attributes i.e. mathematical ability and\ngeneral ability.\nSolution:\nHo: There is no association between attributes.\nH1: There is association.\nii. We choose α = 0.05\niii. Test statistic to be used here is\nawaz e in\n=(-) with v=(r-1) (c-1) d.f\nx2 (0, )2\niv. Calculation\n(Ou)\nMathematical\nGood Fair\nPoor\nTotal\nability\nBi\nB2\nB3\nGeneral ability\nGood\nFair\nPoor\n८११\n44\n82\n44\n170\nA2\n265\n257\n178\n700\nA3\n41\n91\n98\n230\nTotal\n350\n430\n320\n1100\nThe expected frequencies are obtained by multiplying respective rows and columns totals and are divided by total sample size n as given in the table.\n(eu)\nMathematical ability\nGood Bi\nFair B\nGeneral ability\nPoor By\nTotal\n170x350\n170x430\nGood\nAi\n54.09\n170x320\n66.45\n49.45\n169.99\n1100\n1100\n1100\n700x350\n700x430\n700x320\n-222.73\n-=273.64\nFair\nA2\n203.63\n1100\n1100\n1100\n700.01\n230x350\n230x430\n230x320\nPoor\nA1\n-73.18\n-89.91\n66.91 230\n1100\n1100\n1100\nTotal\n350\n430\n320\n1100\nNow test statistic value is computed as;\ney\n(Ou-en)\n(O1-eu)2\n(0, - €)2\neu\n44\n54.09\n- 10.09\n101.81\n1.882\n265\n222.73\n42.27\n1786.75\n8.022\n41\n73.18 -32.18\n1035.55\n14.151\n82\n66.45 15.55\n241.80\n3.639\n257\n273.64 -16.64\n276.8896\n1.012\n91\n89.91\n1.09\n1.188\n0.013\n+44\n49.45 -5.45\n29.70\n0.601\n178\n203.63 -25.64\n657.41\n3.228\n98\n66.91\n31.09\n966.59\n14.446\n1100\n1100\nx=46.994\n2801\n=\n1j.\nv. Critical region\n=46.994\nReject Ho if xx, whereas from\nα-0.05\n0\n9.49\ntable 8.1 of distribution, we have\nXas = Zoos.13-13-1)= X 005.4 = 9.49 x2\nvi. Conclusion\nSince our computed value of chi-square lies in the rejection region, therefore, we reject Ho and conclude that there is association between. mathematical ability and general ability.\nZ\n8.2.2 Shortcut method of calculating 72 in case of (2 x 2) contingency table\nWhen we have (2 x 2) contingency table having four cell frequencies as given below;\nAttributes\nB\nB\nTotal\nA\na\nb\na+b\nQ\nC\nd\nc+d\nTotal\na+c\nb+d\na+b+c+d = n\nThe value of can be calculated directly without computing the expected frequencies by using the following formula\nx2=",
